  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0 \\\\ b \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w.$ What is the value of $a-b?$",
  "worked_solution": "Given that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v=0 \\\\ u\\cdot w=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation $u\\cdot v=0.$ Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 3+b\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2) & =0 \\\\ a+0+b-2 & =0 \\\\ a+b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation $u\\cdot w=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 1+b\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 5 & =0 \\\\ -a+0+2b+5 & =0 \\\\ -a+2b & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}a+b=2 \\\\ -a+2b=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the first equation to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n3b=-3\\,\u27f9\\,b=-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $b=-1$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+(-1) & =2 \\\\ a & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na-b=3-(-1)=4.\n$$",
